WEBVTT

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:20.520
Dr. Walcott speaking.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:22.380
Who?

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:23.950
Mr. William Cornish.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:25.090
Sure.

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:25.990
Send him right up.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:27.070
Thank you.

00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:10.130
Bill, you old son of a gun.

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:11.470
Hello, Steve.

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:12.390
How's everything?

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:13.170
Fine.

00:02:13.490 --> 00:02:14.990
You're a psych for sore eyes.

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:15.850
Come on in.

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:18.360
How long has it been now?

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:19.250
Four years.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:20.860
You haven't changed any.

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:22.080
I'm getting balder.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:23.600
And famous.

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:26.250
New York's most prominent nerve specialist.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:28.020
Happy to know you.

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:28.440
Yeah.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:29.980
You've been...

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.680
crashing into print yourself yeah that's not so good for a dick is it i don't know i imagine

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:40.220
there are a lot of these investigators who wouldn't object to being spread all over the

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:46.610
front page like you've been for the past week no i suppose not but publicity spoils my game

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:51.720
i work best in the dark and for the love of the game eh have you been at it ever since

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:58.440
yeah i can't get out of it too many things to snoop into and i'm an incurable snooper

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:08.630
Yes, I bought those for the convention. They're pretty powerful here. Take a look through these

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:38.190
Where'd you get them?

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:40.280
I had them made in Germany.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:42.500
They're the most powerful glasses I've ever seen.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:44.160
Yeah, they're pretty potent, aren't they?

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:45.540
I always carry them.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:46.720
I use them in my business.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:47.240
How?

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:48.560
Always.

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:51.880
You told me over the telephone you had a surprise for me.

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:52.860
What is it?

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:53.680
Oh, that?

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:56.100
That's something in your line.

00:03:59.560 --> 00:03:59.980
What do you make of it?

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.560
of that. I should say that was a human

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:07.060
ear bone. Exactly.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:10.970
Where'd you get it? I found it last night

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:15.740
in the fireplace over there. You mean to say

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:17.540
rather unusual, isn't it?

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:20.400
Finding the ear bone of a human being in the fireplace

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:21.500
of a fashionable hotel.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:26.500
What the scheme?

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:41.100
Steve. Hello. Send a doctor up real quick.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:43.950
Mr. Walcott is ill. Right away, sir.

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:49.600
Oh, Mr. Gordon.

00:04:56.870 --> 00:04:58.830
Someone just telephoned from apartment A.

00:04:59.010 --> 00:04:59.770
He wants a doctor.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:02.640
Something has happened to Dr. Walcott.

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:09.560
Get me Dr. Bronson.

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:15.970
Well, doctor, something has happened to Dr. Walcott in Apartment A.

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:17.800
You'd better go right up.

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:24.250
Uh, watch the deaths, will you, Joe?

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:59.970
what's the matter mr walcott there he is over there on the dive in no it's nothing serious a little rush of blood to the

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:07.700
head probably vertigo is he subject to these attacks I don't know I haven't

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:14.090
seen him in four years what about that blood on his father oh oh that's nothing

00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:29.360
bumped his head as he fell most likely most likely coming around how is it old fellow

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:30.780
What?

00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:34.250
What happened?

00:06:35.410 --> 00:06:36.590
Why, you fainted.

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:38.580
The heat or something.

00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:41.670
Better be careful of your diet in this weather.

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:43.390
Well, I'll be darned.

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:48.150
And what the...

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:50.540
Oh, you bumped your head.

00:06:52.890 --> 00:06:53.450
That's nothing.

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:56.200
A piece of cork plaster will fix that.

00:06:57.880 --> 00:06:59.000
Ever suffered from vertigo?

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:02.860
No, nothing's ever the matter with me.

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:06.560
Well, you'd better stay quiet for the rest of the night.

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:09.840
Play pinochle with Cornish here.

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:12.150
Always tell him a minute, will you?

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:17.300
Yeah, but Mr. Cornish and I were just going out to get a bite to eat.

00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:19.010
Well, suit yourself.

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:21.780
I'd advise staying quiet myself.

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:24.940
Your liver's a bit off, maybe, huh?

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:27.090
Go on, I haven't any liver.

00:07:27.730 --> 00:07:29.610
Well, maybe that's what's the matter with you.

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:32.240
Well, whatever it is, I'm quite ashamed of myself.

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:34.820
Just put your charge on my bill, Doctor.

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:36.400
Ah, don't be absurd.

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:38.220
Professional courtesy, my boy.

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:40.650
All set?

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:42.560
Well, thanks for coming up.

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:44.320
I feel very important.

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:46.070
You are important, Doctor.

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:47.860
I don't have to tell you that.

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:50.160
My advice is to take things easily for a while.

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:51.140
Good night.

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:52.180
Good night.

00:07:52.370 --> 00:07:52.970
Good night, gentlemen.

00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:05.470
Take his advice and get shot at some more.

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:06.580
Shot at?

00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:08.150
That's what I said.

00:08:09.010 --> 00:08:09.990
What are you talking about?

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:12.810
You were most certainly shot at right through that window.

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:21.640
Well, you are.

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:26.360
Yes, the same old story.

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:35.340
It ricocheted off that wall.

00:08:38.850 --> 00:08:41.090
They're not decorating rooms with bullet marks.

00:08:42.170 --> 00:08:44.090
What's more, the people know all about it.

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:45.620
Are you crazy, Bill?

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:47.490
I've been accused of it.

00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:49.560
Why in the world should anyone shoot at me?

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:55.250
For that matter, why should burned ear bones be found in fireplaces of respectable hotels?

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:58.500
Yeah, but how could they?

00:08:58.750 --> 00:08:59.670
The window's closed.

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:02.110
Sure, the hotel clerk closed it.

00:09:02.380 --> 00:09:03.380
I saw him do it.

00:09:03.770 --> 00:09:05.590
That's what makes me think the management knows.

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:08.740
Bronson and his upset liver.

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:13.020
I wonder if they'd take another shot at you if they got a chance.

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:15.440
Oh, don't worry.

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:18.360
They can't see you in here from the outside with the window closed.

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:19.680
Who can't?

00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:21.520
How should I know?

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:27.980
What are you going to do?

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:28.650
Do?

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:29.980
What can we do?

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:31.920
Any news from that assistant of kind?

00:09:32.180 --> 00:09:32.860
Nothing today.

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:37.000
A great detective force, a hotel clerk, and an undertaker's assistant.

00:09:37.180 --> 00:09:39.800
I told you you'd better call in a firm of regular detectives.

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:44.490
Yes, that would be brilliant, under the circumstances.

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:47.980
But, Bill, I tell you, I haven't an enemy in the world.

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:51.500
People don't go around just shooting other people through hotel windows.

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:58.850
No, neither do doctors find burnt earphones in fireplaces.

00:10:01.210 --> 00:10:02.810
Remarkable likeness, I should say.

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:06.140
Now you stand over there out of the way.

00:10:06.730 --> 00:10:09.970
Better still, take those glasses of yours and watch the street.

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:12.220
Take a good look at every parked car you see.

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:34.480
Bill, I honestly think you're out of your head.

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:40.240
Possibly. Nevertheless, I think...

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:47.460
Did you hear a shot when I fell?

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:51.200
No, but there are such things as Maxim Silenson.

00:10:51.930 --> 00:10:54.870
I doubt anyhow if the shot would be heard above the traffic noises.

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:58.110
Ah, it's absurd. No one would...

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:03.840
No. Take a look at that.

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:06.430
Well, I'll be...

00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:17.390
...downstairs. Have you got a gun?

00:11:17.780 --> 00:11:18.740
Yes, but...

00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:19.440
Well, bring it.

00:11:22.060 --> 00:11:24.500
Do I have to have a gun to interview the hotel management?

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:27.190
We're not going to interview the hotel management.

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:27.840
Yes.

00:11:28.330 --> 00:11:29.870
We're going to interview the people in the...

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:34.130
floor front, the third house from the corner on the opposite side of the street. That's

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:39.960
where the shot came from. You saw it? As plain as I see you. But listen here, Bill. This

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:45.020
is a matter for the police. I saw all the shooting I wanted to see during the war. Now, don't

00:11:45.020 --> 00:11:50.400
worry. There won't be anybody there when we arrive. Besides, I am the police. Come on.

00:11:50.660 --> 00:11:57.480
It'll be much more interesting than the midnight supper. Yeah, and much more dangerous. Just

00:11:57.480 --> 00:11:58.900
a minute, and I'll be right with you.

00:12:23.870 --> 00:12:25.190
I thought. Empty.

00:12:26.140 --> 00:12:26.980
They open everything?

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:28.060
Just about.

00:12:40.330 --> 00:12:41.610
Are you sure we're in the right place?

00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:43.870
You know, powder marks when you see them?

00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:46.870
Looks like it, doesn't it?

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:48.080
Now, don't touch anything.

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:50.300
I'm going to come back here later for fingerprints.

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:51.400
Fingerprints?

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:54.040
I want to see if there's anyone with a record mixed up in it.

00:14:20.570 --> 00:14:21.610
Will you call me a taxi, please?

00:14:23.260 --> 00:14:25.840
There's one outside. I'll be right down.

00:14:26.020 --> 00:14:26.280
Thank you.

00:14:35.950 --> 00:14:37.830
I certainly would like to know what was in that note.

00:14:39.500 --> 00:14:40.280
Supposedly follow her.

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:41.540
Well, it isn't necessary.

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:44.550
We'll see what she has to say to the taxi driver.

00:15:08.670 --> 00:15:10.450
She's going to 241 Wellington Place.

00:15:10.670 --> 00:15:11.550
Well, how do you know?

00:15:11.820 --> 00:15:12.700
I read her lips.

00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:14.700
I learned lip reading years ago.

00:15:15.170 --> 00:15:16.650
That's why I had these lenses made.

00:15:17.430 --> 00:15:19.910
I can make out what anyone is saying a block away.

00:15:20.190 --> 00:15:21.670
Well, I'll be...

00:15:21.670 --> 00:15:23.930
You'd be surprised how handy it comes into my business.

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:25.930
Yes, but weren't you taking an awful chance?

00:15:26.030 --> 00:15:28.850
Suppose she'd gotten into the taxi before she gave the driver the address.

00:15:29.150 --> 00:15:29.970
I wouldn't make any difference.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:32.480
If I had the taxi number, I could have gotten the address later.

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:34.420
Yeah, well, what are we going to do now? Go there?

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:37.300
No, we'll go over to the hotel and have a talk with Gordon.

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:39.920
There's something very funny in the air.

00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:43.350
Now, listen, don't you say anything about having been here.

00:15:43.940 --> 00:15:46.300
In fact, don't say anything about anything.

00:15:46.680 --> 00:15:48.180
In other words, keep my mouth shut.

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:50.340
We'll go out the way we came in.

00:15:57.340 --> 00:15:58.800
Walcott and Cornish are outside.

00:15:59.020 --> 00:15:59.980
They say they want to see you.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:10.620
Wouldn't mind that fellow Walcott but Cornish makes me nervous they say what they wanted no just said they wanted to talk to you

00:16:11.120 --> 00:16:27.210
Well, I suppose I'd better feel any you stick around I may need you all right gentlemen go right in let me do the talk

00:16:41.710 --> 00:16:43.730
Well, gentlemen, what can I do for you?

00:16:47.890 --> 00:16:48.650
Any bad effects?

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:51.640
No, I'm quite all right.

00:16:52.780 --> 00:16:56.110
I thought you would be, just in touch with the heat.

00:16:56.750 --> 00:16:59.980
I should think you two would pass out in here without a window open.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:05.010
What's the matter you afraid of a little fresh air? I haven't noticed that it was close

00:17:05.740 --> 00:17:14.970
Sometimes it's cooler in here with the windows closed like plenty of fresh air

00:17:23.600 --> 00:17:29.920
They've got any idea why we're here not the slightest well is to tell you that we think

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:36.240
I think, or rather I think, that Bronson's theory about what was wrong with Walcott is a lot of bunk.

00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:41.050
Really? Really. You know what happened to him?

00:17:42.760 --> 00:17:43.980
Why... He was shot.

00:17:44.180 --> 00:17:44.400
Shot?

00:17:45.280 --> 00:17:47.780
Just that. Or rather, shot at.

00:17:48.470 --> 00:17:51.370
Why should anyone shoot into a hotel room at midnight?

00:17:52.360 --> 00:17:57.250
That cut on his forehead is a bullet wound. Somebody took a pot shot at him through the window.

00:17:57.390 --> 00:17:58.130
Oh, absurd.

00:17:58.680 --> 00:17:59.960
Why should anyone...

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:06.170
shoot at Dr. Walcott? I don't know. That's what we're here to talk over with you. But what makes

00:18:06.170 --> 00:18:12.450
you think all this? Walcott is a healthy man. He's not going around flopping on the floor just

00:18:12.450 --> 00:18:17.910
because there's a little heat. That's a bullet wound over his father. I never saw one. Is that

00:18:17.910 --> 00:18:23.470
your only reason for thinking he was shot at it? That's enough. What do you think, Dr. Walcott?

00:18:24.090 --> 00:18:29.920
I? I don't know what to think. Have you any enemies? Anyone who'd shoot?

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:35.870
at you? No. Did you hear a shot, Mr. Cornish? No, but the traffic... Well, look here, this thing is

00:18:35.870 --> 00:18:42.400
getting serious. Oh, I can't help but think you're mistaken, Mr. Cornish. Why, it's a, it's a terrible

00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:47.540
thing to contemplate. We'll have the matter up into, of course. I'll have the house detective write

00:18:47.540 --> 00:18:55.990
up. I said before, gentlemen, I think you're mistaken. And, uh, would it be too much to ask you

00:18:55.990 --> 00:18:59.590
to say nothing about your fears outside for the time being?

00:19:00.410 --> 00:19:02.050
Of course, we'll attend to the matter right away,

00:19:02.210 --> 00:19:04.600
but if this thing ever got into the newspapers,

00:19:04.820 --> 00:19:07.160
why, you can imagine what it would do to the hotel.

00:19:08.210 --> 00:19:09.870
The business is just coming back,

00:19:09.990 --> 00:19:13.420
but if my guests ever suspected that they were being shot at

00:19:13.420 --> 00:19:14.880
through the hotel window, you...

00:19:16.150 --> 00:19:16.830
Well, of course.

00:19:17.390 --> 00:19:19.070
I think Mr. Walcott understands.

00:19:19.430 --> 00:19:20.210
How about it, Steve?

00:19:20.780 --> 00:19:21.700
Why, yes, of course.

00:19:23.320 --> 00:19:25.120
In the meantime, he'd like to change his room.

00:19:25.410 --> 00:19:26.350
Yes, I'd like that.

00:19:26.760 --> 00:19:29.610
Oh, most certainly, but I still think you're mistaken.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:31.580
Boy, it's too monstrous.

00:19:32.220 --> 00:19:34.400
Of course, the least we can do is to change your room for you.

00:19:35.010 --> 00:19:37.510
Is there any special location you'd like?

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:38.900
An inside room.

00:19:39.280 --> 00:19:40.740
Something looking out on the court.

00:19:41.710 --> 00:19:42.830
Something on the first floor.

00:19:43.540 --> 00:19:44.580
I'm staying with him.

00:19:45.300 --> 00:19:47.660
He's not taking any chances from now on.

00:19:48.550 --> 00:19:49.690
First floor on the court.

00:19:50.870 --> 00:19:53.370
Well, they're not very comfortable on a hot night,

00:19:53.430 --> 00:19:59.860
but I think Sweet G over there across the court is available.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:01.470
Would you like that?

00:20:03.640 --> 00:20:04.600
That'll do very well.

00:20:05.780 --> 00:20:07.260
All right, I'll attend to it right away.

00:20:09.250 --> 00:20:10.350
Get me Mr. Rankin, please.

00:20:11.990 --> 00:20:16.940
Oh, Mr. Rankin, Dr. Walcott is changing his rooms to Sweet G across the court.

00:20:17.640 --> 00:20:19.360
See that these things are moved at once, please.

00:20:20.400 --> 00:20:20.560
Yes.

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:21.840
Hey, boy.

00:20:25.350 --> 00:20:25.950
Thank you, doctor.

00:20:29.090 --> 00:20:29.270
Boy.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:31.380
You caught on to the gag immediately.

00:20:31.910 --> 00:20:33.250
I don't know that I did.

00:20:33.740 --> 00:20:36.400
I know for some purpose or other you stepped on my foot.

00:20:36.650 --> 00:20:37.830
I almost yelped.

00:20:38.680 --> 00:20:43.270
I wanted you to take this room so we could find out what our good friends across the court

00:20:43.270 --> 00:20:44.730
talk about when we're not there.

00:21:24.800 --> 00:21:25.600
Put down this number.

00:21:28.750 --> 00:21:29.130
Capital.

00:21:30.700 --> 00:21:53.330
six seven five three things are bad if you must know it off

00:21:53.330 --> 00:21:58.130
has been another attempt this time in a prominent New York doctor

00:21:58.800 --> 00:21:59.920
Walcock the nurse

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:05.320
specialist. And that fellow, William Cornish, the investigator that you've been reading about so much

00:22:05.320 --> 00:22:12.180
in the newspapers, is mixed up in the cave. Cornish? What's he got to do with it? Nothing yet,

00:22:12.850 --> 00:22:19.500
but he's a friend of Walcott's, and he has me worried. Any clues? No, not yet. When the Countess

00:22:19.500 --> 00:22:25.180
Gobert was driven out at midnight last week, Rankin was sure that they were shooting from a car

00:22:25.180 --> 00:22:29.730
that was parked across the street. But he's changed his mind now, and...

00:22:30.350 --> 00:22:31.550
It has nothing more to suggest.

00:22:32.750 --> 00:22:33.550
Rankin's a fool.

00:22:34.040 --> 00:22:35.160
He's doing his best.

00:22:35.980 --> 00:22:38.380
You can't expect a hotel clerk to be much of a detective.

00:22:41.020 --> 00:22:43.020
Go to a regular firm of detectives.

00:22:43.500 --> 00:22:45.480
That would be a bright idea, wouldn't it?

00:22:46.790 --> 00:22:49.110
They'd find out about the other.

